WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.180
- Happy November, everybody.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.460
Welcome to the Data
Science Hangout.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.710
I actually just shared
our 100th Data Science Hangout

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:23.000
recording to YouTube yesterday and
made me realize that we

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.350
never actually celebrated that.
So thank you all for being

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.690
here and making this
space what it is.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.959
I can't believe we're
already over 100

00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:33.860
Data Science Hangouts.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:38.510
If this is your first time
joining us, hi, so nice to meet

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.470
you. I'm Rachael. I lead
customer marketing at Posit.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:46.200
This is our open space
chat about data science leadership,

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.610
questions you're facing,

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.940
and getting to hear about
what's going on in the world of

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.230
data across
different industries.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.850
So we're here every Thursday
at the same time, same place.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.420
So if you're watching
this recording on YouTube later,

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:00.900
and you want to join us live,

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.690
you can use the link below
to add it to your calendar.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:05.870
But at the Hangout,

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:09.200
we're all dedicated to
making this a welcoming environment

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.970
for everybody. Love
to hear from everyone,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.080
no matter your years
of experience, your titles,

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.080
industry, or even the
languages that you work in.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.510
It's totally OK to just listen
in here. And I say if you're

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:27.200
out walking the dog
maybe, you're at lunch,

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.580
it's OK to just listen in.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.520
But there's also three ways to
jump in and ask questions or

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.090
add your own perspective. So
you can raise your hand on

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.010
Zoom, and I'll keep
an eye out here.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.110
You can put questions
in the Zoom chat.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.960
And just put a little star next
to it if it's something you

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.980
want me to read out loud
and said. And then lastly, we have

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.250
a Slido link where you can
ask questions anonymously too.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.640
And thank you, Curtis, for
sharing that there in the chat.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:59.070
Sometimes I forget to say this,
but if anybody is hiring, feel

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.820
free to share any open
roles in the chat as well.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.830
That's definitely not
spammy at all to me.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.805
I think it's great to share
those jobs in the chat too.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:10.650
But with that,

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:15.510
I am so excited to introduce
my co-host for today David Sluder.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:19.770
David is data science senior
program manager at the

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:23.580
Institute of Nuclear Power
Operations. And, David,

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.700
I'd love to have you just
introduce yourself a bit and

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.400
share a little bit about
your role but also something you

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.855
like to do outside of work too.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:32.660
- Sure.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.830
Thank you, Rachel. And good
morning, good afternoon,

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.065
good evening, depending
on where you are.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.150
I'm really excited to be here.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.810
I've got two disclaimers though I
have to start off with, right?

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.230
So number one, I just
want to publicly say,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:47.910
I'm here to represent
my own opinion.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.310
I am not here to
represent my employer,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.080
Institute of Nuclear
Power Operations,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.450
or the broader nuclear
industry. You know, of course,

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.520
I'm going to talk about them
to give the conversation some

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.280
context. But this is
purely my opinion.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.880
And then the second piece is
that I really need to make sure

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.925
everyone knows that anything
I talk about today is--

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.860
it's not just my work. So I
work as part of a broader team.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.030
And the team has worked so
hard to build out data science at

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.060
INPO. I work with a bunch
of smart passionate people.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:18.795
And it's just really

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.730
important that they get
a lot of credit too here.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:23.970
So with that said,

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:28.110
my name is David Sluder. I'm
a data scientist here at INPO.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:33.610
I've been at INPO for almost
12 years now, only about 2 years

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:34.570
in the data science space,

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:37.480
about 10 years in the
IT space here at INPO.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:38.790
And

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:40.380
it's

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.520
a really interesting company.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:47.110
And I didn't know anything about
INPO until I started to work here.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.410
So maybe I'll give you a
briefer on what we are and what

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:55.570
we do. So INPO is an
independent non-profit that

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:57.730
is funded by our members.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.860
And our members are the
nuclear power industry.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:04.075
So within the US, I think
we have 54 nuclear power stations.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.405
And they all kind of pay
us a member based fee

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:09.820
to perform our service.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.085
And now our service is that we

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:16.420
set and assess safety and
reliability standards for the

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.740
nuclear power industry. So we
set these standards. We send

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:24.430
out evaluation teams to
actually kind of determine how

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:27.250
well the different stations are
adhering to these standards. We

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.032
kind of give them a score
at the very end of that.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.650
And we do that on
a regular cadence.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:37.870
So we do that along with
also kind of facilitating the

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:39.593
sharing of knowledge
across stations,

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.420
which is really interesting
in an industry where you have

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.310
different companies
competing together,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.360
but then also sharing
information with an independent

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.680
organization that
allows them to share

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.300
what they've learned so that
we're all able to learn from

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.390
each other's experiences,
mistakes, challenges,

00:04:58.390 --> 00:04:59.035
that sort of thing.

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:06.085
I've been doing kind of data
science for about two years now.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:08.500
Before that, I worked on IT.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:12.310
But my background
is my bachelor's.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.740
You can call it my
background is in humanistic psychology

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:18.350
which is the most qualitative
thing that you could do.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:21.625
So it's been a fun road
to figure out how I got here.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.440
Outside of work, I like
to do a lot of things.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.930
I like to read. I like to
laugh. And I like to travel.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:32.860
So this past weekend, I was
able to indulge in all three.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:35.320
You know, Posit Con for
this year was in Chicago.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.290
And it was the first time I
had been to Chicago with any chance

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:42.010
to explore it. So I got back
home, and I told my wife,

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:43.360
Katie, hey, we need
to go to Chicago.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.400
So we went to Chicago
this past weekend.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.160
And we saw our
favorite comedian.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.050
We went to go see some improv.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.150
We found kind of
a punk bookstore,

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:53.500
which was a whole lot of fun.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:57.640
And we got to do just a lot
of wandering around the city.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:01.300
So it was an awesome trip.
And I, at this point,

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:02.960
kind recommend
Chicago to anyone,

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:05.350
especially if it's the
weekend before Halloween because then

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:08.410
you just get to ride around
on the L train and watch

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:09.710
everyone's costumes.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:12.380
- That's awesome.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:16.070
I know there were a few people
from Posit who have been

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:19.205
thinking about moving there after
going for Posit Conference too.

00:06:19.925 --> 00:06:21.200
- I see why. Yeah.

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.065
- I see George asked,
who did you see perform?

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:26.705
- Maria Bamford. She's

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.975
my favorite comedian.
Number two is Kyle Cunane.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:31.800
- Well, David,

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.580
thanks so much for the intro
and sharing a little bit about

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:36.350
your background there. I know,

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:39.590
when we were chatting you
shared some more info on how

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:43.790
you had previously worked in
IT as well. And so I was just

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.140
curious to kick off the
conversation with that because

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.800
that's something that comes
up quite a bit in this space.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:52.850
But having moved from IT to
data science, I know you have

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:55.550
probably pretty unique
perspectives on both sides.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:59.240
What would you recommend
to some of us who might be

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.270
struggling to communicate
across those lines?

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:03.420
- That's a good question.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.890
And I think a challenge that
I hear in the Hangouts and then

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.635
also just kind of conversations
I've had with other people.

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:10.690
To

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:15.750
me, a lot of times, you
have these challenges.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.380
And it really just kind of
boils down to building a good

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:19.460
relationship.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.550
So that can

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:23.700
pan out in a lot
of different ways.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.320
And it's really dependent
on your organization.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:29.690
And I think how it's set up and
just kind of the general size of it.

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:32.720
The way that we did it was

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:34.420
we

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:39.410
kind of built data science
not quite from scratch.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:44.690
But we had one organization at INPO
that did some sort of data science-y work.

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:49.190
And then our senior leadership team
kind of identified it as a

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:51.680
strategic priority for the
company to actually build out

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:52.790
that capability.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.580
So then there was a lot of
emphasis and a lot of focus on

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:59.600
it. And that was right after
I had moved into the data science

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.490
organization. So we

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:05.240
knew that data science and
IT needed to work together

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:06.590
because, I mean,

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:09.620
every single project that we work
on has an IT component to

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:11.040
it. So

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:13.115
some of what we did is

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:15.570
we started having

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:18.770
leadership meetings between data
science leadership and IT

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:22.280
leadership on some sort of
regular cadence just to talk about

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:25.700
what the roadmaps look like,
what projects are on the

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:27.920
horizon, what challenges
there might be,

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:31.100
and just kind of putting
everything out in the open as

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.500
much as you can just to
have those conversations.

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:35.255
On a more

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:36.990
personal level,

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:40.920
I think it's really important
for the two groups to learn

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.445
more about what
each group does.

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:48.080
So sit down with a
network engineer and understand why

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.010
they might be a little cautious
about opening up all of--

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:52.510
opening

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:58.680
up their infrastructure to all of
the packages on a repo or having

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:00.530
a network engineer,

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:02.540
sit down with a data
scientist and understand why that's a

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:03.560
challenge for them.

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:08.540
It really, to me, just
comes down to having conversations

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:13.230
and being open, and honest,
and transparent, and figuring out a

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:14.940
way to move forward together.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:15.440
- That's great.

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:19.860
How did you actually
first start those conversations?

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:22.020
I think you said it was at
the Sr. Leadership level.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.780
But was there something that
kind of drove those initial

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:25.950
conversations?

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:27.650
- Well, so

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:33.690
data science kind of became
an emphasis for INPO because the

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:36.690
way that we were doing
business was changing. So we

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.210
go and evaluate stations
every two years.

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:42.630
That's kind of been the regular
cadence for about 30 years.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:47.130
But a lot can happen in between
those two-year assessments,

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:51.120
right? So we understood that we
needed to get into more of a

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.250
continuous monitoring
sort of fashion.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:56.070
And the only way that we can
do that because we don't want to

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:57.690
go out and evaluate more often,

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:00.390
it's a really costly thing to
set up an evaluation team and

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:02.970
send it to a
station. So instead,

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:05.610
it meant that we needed to
figure out a way to use the

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:08.820
data that we have to do some
modeling, some analysis,

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.730
and try to understand what
their quote-unquote performance

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:13.680
looks like in between
those evaluations.

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:17.910
So that was kind of where
it landed at a strategic level.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:19.890
And then it kind of became

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:23.250
our job to actually
implement that. And

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:27.450
recently,

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:30.810
it was real lucky having moved
right from IT to data science

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:33.270
and being able to bring
that perspective to both sides,

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:36.880
I think, put us in a
really good situation.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.525
But I know that's kind of
a rare situation to be in too.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:42.660
So I think,

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.750
yeah, just kind of
like sitting down,

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.910
putting your chairs in
a circle, and saying, what's

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:49.200
going on? What can we work on?

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.330
How do we figure this
all out together?

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.775
Is just kind of the way
the right way to start.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:55.185
- Yeah. Thank you.

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:58.000
Russ, I see you asked
a question in the chat.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.100
Do you want to jump in here?

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.300
- Yeah. Thank you.

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:05.510
I have a master's in
counseling I love humanistic psychology.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:06.810
I could talk to you
about that all day.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.170
How does that help you
in data science? If so,

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:10.460
can you say
something about that?

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:16.100
- I thought about that kind
of in preparation for that talk.

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:18.080
And the more that
I thought about it,

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.150
the more kind of threads
that I could see.

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:24.470
Just because a lot of data
science and a lot of working in

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:27.590
an organization in general
is kind of working on

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:29.615
relationships and
communication. And

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:32.570
actually, actively

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:36.410
listening, that was something I
remember taking a class on that

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:40.710
when I was in college.
And I feel like I still

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:44.210
try to use those skills to
this day where you're actually

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.970
sitting there in front of a
person and you're hearing what they say,

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:50.150
and you're thinking about what
they say instead of figuring

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:51.920
out what you're just
going to say next.

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:55.910
So kind of having a
real active listening sort of conversation,

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:58.800
and then understanding
that people

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:04.380
are people, and we can
be inconsistent and hard to

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.975
understand, and really

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:11.640
all it takes is maybe a
little bit of openness and kindness to

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:16.395
give space to the sorts
of conversations that need to happen,

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:19.950
the sorts of crucial
conversations that we have--

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:20.610
we call it,

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:23.970
that's kind of a term that we
use at INPO for these sort of

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:28.350
intense conversations that need
some sort of resolution. And

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:30.540
in my opinion,

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:34.260
just kind of being open
to that and understanding that there's

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:35.850
going to be some
emotional reactions.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.860
And that's a-OK because
we're all human beings.

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:45.060
But it's important to not just kind of
react to them and just fan the flames.

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:48.360
You need to figure out what the
sources of the issue and address that.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:51.430
- Love that. Thank you. Alan,
I see you have a question here.

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:52.230
You want to jump in?

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:53.175
- Yeah, sure. Hey.

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:55.500
Hey, David. I'm really curious,

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:59.440
so my instinct is that
performance metrics in nuclear

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.710
power is really, really specific,
like a super specific domain.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:04.390
If that's the case,

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:08.110
I wonder how that domain
expertise comes to play in the

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.570
stuff you do day to day or
if maybe that's the wrong

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:11.545
assumption and really

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:15.580
the metrics are around
efficiency, and people metrics,

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:17.140
and things that are
really familiar.

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.420
Like I wonder how specific
what you're doing is to

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:21.500
that industry and

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:24.810
how you operate in that
kind of environment?

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:28.510
- That's an excellent question.
So the industry metrics we get,

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:33.680
we get hundreds of data points
from every station every month. And

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:36.430
we use that to--

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:36.940
we end up--

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:39.560
because every station can
be a little different.

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:42.860
You've got to go through
some sort of normalization scheme.

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:46.210
So we have these
indicators that are a way--

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:48.130
that we normalize the data.

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:50.395
And then that's what we
can actually do some modeling on.

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:54.970
And I have learned just
how little I know about nuclear

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:58.480
power by moving to the industry
side of things and needing to

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:02.680
understand exactly what every
label, every acronym means.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:06.070
And it will take a really long
time to get to that point.

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:09.210
And luckily, I don't need all of
that to do my day to day job.

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:13.480
But whenever I'm working on
a really important model,

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:15.670
I've got to make sure that
I'm not making some really bad

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:16.690
assumptions about it.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:21.520
So that means that I got to
sit down and talk with my manager, director,

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:24.160
or some other subject matter
experts in some of the

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:26.567
functional areas here at
INPO just to kind of make

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:28.570
sure that

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:31.510
whatever I don't know isn't
going to actually bite me

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:34.345
sometime. So yeah,
that's a great comment.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:39.850
- Thanks. I know you mentioned
data science is relatively

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:40.915
new for INPO.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:46.270
How have you been successful
in introducing data science and

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:47.200
data science tools?

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:52.220
- Great question. So

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:54.728
introducing

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:59.850
a new tool to the enterprise
is a really challenging thing.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:02.750
You can't just say here
are some model results and expect

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:06.590
everyone to understand what that
means. So one of the

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.540
changes that we've made.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:11.660
And it's actually kind of
part of our governance that we've

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:16.340
written is that anytime there
is a new tool of some sort

00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:18.260
delivered, we have to

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:23.235
figure out a way to
explain it or interpret it

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:25.140
with some

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.140
other tool or maybe kind of
a help function of some sort.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:31.380
So one of the things that

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:36.660
we've learned is that
delivering these model results

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:37.980
that require some

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:41.820
subtlety and understanding
is hard to do for people that

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.957
don't use it on a
real frequent basis.

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:49.530
So we've added a lot more
kind of like help functions into the

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.230
tools that we build the
way that we expose these model

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:56.670
results. And then we've also,
of built some other tools to--

00:15:58.800 --> 00:15:59.940
I got to talk generally,

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:02.670
to help you understand why
the model is saying what it's

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:06.660
saying. And we've had
a lot of success in

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:12.060
thinking about things from
the end user perspective and

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:15.570
not at the data science perspective
to help us figure out exactly

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:18.270
what we can do better to

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:21.460
make sure that they use the
tool correctly and they get the

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:22.870
insight that they
want out of it.

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:23.650
- Yes.

00:16:23.650 --> 00:16:27.070
Kind of maybe this follows
along with this a little bit

00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:29.470
and no worries if there's
certain things you can't share

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:32.260
with us. But there was an
anonymous question that was,

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:36.130
what kind of data science use
cases can you share with us in

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:38.290
the nuclear industry
and in your company?

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:39.940
- Yeah. So

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:45.280
one I kind of alluded to
earlier is kind of the impetus

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:48.010
for data science at INPO
is trying to understand

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:49.907
performance in between the

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:53.950
assessments every two
years, biannual, biennial,

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:54.880
I never remember the word.

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:00.500
So we get data
from the industry every month.

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:04.760
And we have a model that
helps us gauge that sort of

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:07.640
performance in between
the assessments.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:09.920
So the assessments are kind of
our gold standard and the way

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:11.960
that we know if the
model is right or wrong.

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:17.990
But it gives us an
estimate in between for their performance.

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:20.869
And then from there,

00:17:20.869 --> 00:17:24.290
there are other things where you
can maybe kind of come up

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:29.360
with some risk models to
see if something has a high

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:30.950
probability of happening.

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:32.080
And

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:36.380
obviously, you don't want
it to happen. So we want to

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:39.830
figure out that risk and
try to get ahead of it.

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:42.185
Let's see what else we got.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:44.710
I'm going to stop there just

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:47.135
to make sure I keep it
general enough, sorry.

00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:52.610
- That's OK. So when
you share those models, who

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:57.300
are you sharing them with or
how do you share the model with

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:58.830
broader stakeholder groups?

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:02.210
So data science at INPO,

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:05.100
our department is actually
called data science.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:09.720
And it's composed of three
groups. So one is data

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:12.660
visualization. One is
kind of data analytics.

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:16.350
And that's where I live.
And then one is data quality

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:19.215
or data management. I think
we call it data quality.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:23.540
So whenever I build a model,

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:27.630
I'm just kind of having the
results end up in a database

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:31.830
somewhere. And then we rely
on our incredibly capable data

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:34.650
visualization group to
actually kind of build those

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:37.020
visualizations to deliver
it to an end user.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:41.220
So no one ever really gets
those raw results out of the database.

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:44.940
They get something within a
broader context to help them--

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:48.290
to help make sure that they
are interpreting it correctly.

00:18:49.150 --> 00:18:49.890
- Thank you.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:50.250
- Sure.

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:52.250
- Let me jump over to one other

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:55.320
anonymous question
first here. And one was,

00:18:55.320 --> 00:19:00.270
pharma has a huge emphasis on
a very muddled concept of validation.

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:03.240
Is there something like this
perception of regulatory

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.040
overhead that
affects your work?

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:08.010
- That exists within
the nuclear industry.

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:09.975
But it doesn't affect INPO. So

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:11.940
there is--

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:14.610
I remember when I worked
IT, there's a group,

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:17.100
it's called NITSL, N-I-T-S-L.

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:19.500
The Nuclear Information
Technology and Strategic

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:24.720
Leadership group, which is kind of
a more nuclear IT group, working group.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:29.640
And they have a focus on
quality assurance because there

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:32.190
are very strict
regulations that go into

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:36.090
how you validate that a system,

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:40.125
that like a software system is going to
do what you say it's going to do

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:43.880
as long as it--

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.930
it's going to do what
you say it's going to do,

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:47.250
and it's going to
work correctly,

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:49.890
and there's no way that it
can kind of get it wrong.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:54.360
But that mostly affects the
safety systems at nuclear stations.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:55.380
As far as I understand--

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:57.720
this is obviously
not my wheelhouse.

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:02.360
But because we at INPO
don't work directly with

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:05.450
supporting the safety system
at a nuclear power station,

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:08.610
we don't really have to
go through that very,

00:20:08.610 --> 00:20:09.605
very rigorous process.

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:11.810
- Thank you. George,

00:20:11.810 --> 00:20:15.080
I think this question
might be kind of related.

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:16.130
But I saw you had a question

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:20.510
earlier about safety assessments. Do
you want to jump in?

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:23.940
- Sure. Yeah. Yeah,
I was just curious,

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:29.460
the data that you're producing
and from the assessments,

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:32.200
is that ever shared with
other federal agencies?

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:34.485
And I was thinking of OSHA and

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:39.270
just been thinking more about
how similar to the Nordisk and

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:40.725
their submissions to the FDA?

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:46.215
I'm wondering if you're doing something
like that or if it's even possible.

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:49.200
- We do

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:52.600
share some of the
content from the reports.

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:55.680
Let me make sure that--

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.320
I know the one
place that we share

00:20:59.400 --> 00:20:59.900
the

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:01.320
report, I believe,

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.780
it's the report but
it's definitely the assessment

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:07.395
number, the assessment
score is the company that

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:12.965
issues out insurance to
the nuclear power industry. So

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:18.040
our assessment score
is directly linked to

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:21.050
nuclear stations
insurance rate,

00:21:21.050 --> 00:21:22.555
which gives us a
little bit of--

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:28.710
it makes it very important that
everything is right and that there are

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:33.300
kind of no worries, no challenges
to it that can't be answered.

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:35.940
We share some,

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:39.950
we call it, operating
experience with

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:42.000
some other groups.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:44.830
But I don't think that we
share the actual report with them.

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:46.515
So it's a very tightly

00:21:47.190 --> 00:21:50.370
guarded thing that we share
with the senior leadership at a

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:51.570
station. And

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:55.410
we share at least the
assessment number with the

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:57.900
insurance group. But
for the most part,

00:21:57.900 --> 00:22:01.020
we kind of hold on to the
actual report findings

00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:03.030
because there's

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:07.040
