00:24:33.530 --> 00:24:35.105
- One of the things we
had a lot of success with

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:40.070
in a former life of mine
was when we deployed reports and a

00:24:40.070 --> 00:24:42.270
lot of these reports were
sort of meant to monitor

00:24:43.340 --> 00:24:45.860
model scores over time. And so

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:48.560
this is not helping your
Microsoft issue, I'm sorry. But

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:49.940
in terms of just

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:51.990
use cases in general,

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.980
we would do things like

00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:59.495
query in the back end, we could
run a report every day, every week

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:00.950
perform a query,

00:25:00.950 --> 00:25:03.155
bring in this new data
that we hadn't seen yet,

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:06.905
and then run that through
our models, and then

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:09.260
get some model scores.

00:25:09.260 --> 00:25:11.780
And then we could track those
model scores compared to

00:25:11.780 --> 00:25:13.880
yesterday's, last
week's, whatever it is,

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:16.970
and then we could use
