00:29:02.730 --> 00:29:05.175
That's really
helped a lot for us.

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:09.360
And I think Catherine from
my company is on here too.

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:11.160
I don't know if she has
any additional comments.

00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:11.340
- Yeah.

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:14.590
Javier is totally shameless
about asking tons of questions.

00:29:14.590 --> 00:29:19.990
And sure thing. But I do
think that you're right in asking about

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:23.070
where do you go to get

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:27.330
best practices because,
internally, your team has--

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:30.015
or your company has a
way that they do things.

00:29:30.910 --> 00:29:33.870
So getting exposure to the
ways that other companies do things,

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:37.650
like, knowing what else is
out there can be really difficult.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:42.750
I don't personally do too much
research because I try to not

00:29:42.750 --> 00:29:43.695
work outside of work.

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:47.800
But it does make it--

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:51.000
it does make it difficult to
figure out what else is out

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:53.460
there to then come back to
those conversations if you're

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:56.250
setting up data scientists
to IT or those kinds of

00:29:56.250 --> 00:29:59.070
conversations to bring those ideas if
