00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:25.330
too and having like
smaller conversations or one-off conversations.

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:29.770
I know some people have gotten
to make friends from the Data

00:30:29.770 --> 00:30:30.910
Science Hangouts too.

00:30:30.910 --> 00:30:34.420
So I encourage people to
connect with each other on

00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:37.270
LinkedIn too and feel
free to share your

00:30:37.990 --> 00:30:41.485
LinkedIns in the chat too if
you do want to connect with others.

00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:45.970
But I feel like I should
say this here too.

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:49.420
Something I've been thinking about
is if there are certain

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:53.590
topics that come up in a
forum like this where we maybe want

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:55.150
to dive deeper into it,

00:30:55.870 --> 00:30:59.740
could we have a smaller group
conversation following it.

00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:03.370
Maybe it's on a specific
topic, like, I don't know

00:31:03.970 --> 00:31:04.470
securing

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:07.570
packages or having the
conversation with it about

00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:11.170
using open source packages
or maybe it's about shiny in

00:31:11.170 --> 00:31:12.805
production or
something like that.

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:14.850
So

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:17.650
I'll think a bit
more on that as well.

00:31:19.030 --> 00:31:21.640
- Yeah. The challenge
for us is, we,

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:25.540
at INPO, are
pretty new to this.

00:31:25.540 --> 00:31:29.530
So there isn't that sort
of institutional knowledge that we can

00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:33.190
reach out and question
other people about it. So

00:31:33.910 --> 00:31:34.960
at this point, either

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:37.480
kind of talking to other
people in the nuclear industry.

00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:38.110
And we do have

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:43.270
a data science industry working group
that we've set up at INPO

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:45.040
but across the
nuclear industry.

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:47.815
And we're starting to get
some good traction with that.

00:31:49.210 --> 00:31:52.810
But even past that, it's
good to have conversations with people

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:56.260
in different industries
just to understand

00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:58.690
how they might think
about things differently.

00:31:59.260 --> 00:32:00.370
- Absolutely.

00:32:00.970 --> 00:32:04.450
Justin, I see you had a
question a bit earlier. Justin

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:05.950
Lee, do you want to jump in?

00:32:06.890 --> 00:32:10.270
- Yes, I am looking back to
see what my question was.

00:32:10.270 --> 00:32:11.000
- No worries.

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:13.780
I can ask your question if
you want to add context too. But--

00:32:14.290 --> 00:32:14.790
- Please do.

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:17.860
- The question was,
speaking generally,

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:22.420
how is the industry beginning
to use natural language processing?

00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:24.650
- OK.

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:30.050
I'll tell you the
little that I know. So

00:32:31.070 --> 00:32:32.150
I know that

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:38.195
some places have set up
their own private instances of ChatGPT

00:32:38.900 --> 00:32:40.330
because nuclear

00:32:41.210 --> 00:32:45.170
data is regulated, tightly regulated.
So you have to be

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:48.150
really careful about what
infrastructure it lives on.

00:32:48.150 --> 00:32:49.760
So generally speaking,

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:52.730
we're going to want to keep
things on premise in our own

00:32:52.730 --> 00:32:57.410
domains rather than use something on
Azure or AWS if we can.

00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:01.595
So people have set up
private instances of ChatGPT

00:33:02.210 --> 00:33:04.160
to try to understand how

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:07.110
it could be used for
their organizations.

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:10.910
And there has been some success
but. As you all probably know,

00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:14.430
not everything that comes
out of ChatGPT is correct,

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:16.490
even if it sounds like a
good well-formed sentence.

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:19.370
So there's that. And then a

00:33:20.210 --> 00:33:22.370
lot there's a lot
of interest in

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:25.715
trying to figure out how to

00:33:26.360 --> 00:33:27.395
look at--

00:33:29.270 --> 00:33:32.420
this is a pretty well known
kind of program across

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:34.590
stations. So I don't think
it's wrong to talk about it.

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:37.700
So stations have something
called a corrective action

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:38.935
program. So

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:41.590
kind of generally
speaking, anytime

00:33:42.470 --> 00:33:45.900
there is an event,
something goes wrong,

00:33:45.900 --> 00:33:48.140
whether that's someone
tripping on the sidewalk,

00:33:48.140 --> 00:33:52.355
walking into the station, or
there's a reactor scram, there is

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:55.130
a report written up about that.

00:33:55.130 --> 00:33:56.070
And for

00:33:57.020 --> 00:33:58.250
a single unit station,

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:00.730
I think there's probably 600
people working there. And

00:34:01.670 --> 00:34:04.790
that means there are a lot
of these reports that get written.

00:34:04.790 --> 00:34:05.832
And at

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:07.710
a certain point,

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:11.750
you have a whole lot of
text data and not enough people or

00:34:11.750 --> 00:34:14.510
the right technology to
actually kind of trend it and

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:18.920
track it well. So there's a
lot of interest right now in

00:34:18.920 --> 00:34:23.690
understanding how to use this
kind of history of cap records

00:34:23.690 --> 00:34:26.129
to understand the

00:34:27.110 --> 00:34:29.580
behaviors that might
influence performance.

00:34:31.590 --> 00:34:34.115
So those are the two kind of
big use cases I've heard so far.

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:37.320
- That makes sense. Thank you.

00:34:38.929 --> 00:34:39.650
- Thank you.

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:41.800
Another

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:45.094
question, I think I maybe saw

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:47.900
earlier maybe something
anonymous as well.

00:34:49.699 --> 00:34:51.930
Well, actually, let me
shift gears for a second.

00:34:51.930 --> 00:34:54.230
I know when we were
chatting before,

00:34:54.230 --> 00:34:58.340
you were talking a little bit
about how you're hiring for a role.

00:34:58.340 --> 00:35:01.700
And I thought it might be good
to just discuss a little bit of

00:35:01.700 --> 00:35:05.930
what you've learned recently in trying
to fill a data science role too?

00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:07.430
- It is--

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:11.220
it's challenging, as a
lot of you probably know.

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:13.340
So we did actually
fill the role.

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:18.120
The person hasn't started yet.
But kind of everything's been signed.

00:35:18.120 --> 00:35:19.190
So we're good there.

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:23.060
And I feel like I learned a
lot about what it takes to hire a

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:24.392
data scientist in that process.

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:26.860
So

00:35:27.410 --> 00:35:31.600
we found that we got
a lot of success using

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:36.830
recruiters versus just kind of
having an open job posting just

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:40.350
because the market is
pretty well flooded.

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:43.545
And we wanted someone that
had some particular natural language

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:45.180
experience.

00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:48.250
So instead of going through the

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:50.370
mountain of resumes,

00:35:50.370 --> 00:35:53.400
it was a little bit easier
to have people help us find--

00:35:53.400 --> 00:35:56.520
people who were looking and people
who had the right sort of resume.

00:35:56.520 --> 00:35:58.170
And then from there,

00:35:58.170 --> 00:36:01.780
going through the technical screen to
make sure that they had the

00:36:04.230 --> 00:36:06.555
right understanding that
we were looking for.

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:11.780
And then so that wasn't
too bad. But trying

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:13.260
to find the right--

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:14.700
especially when

00:36:15.910 --> 00:36:19.840
I don't know enough about
natural language processing to

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:21.040
put it on my resume.

00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:24.190
But I'm trying to hire people
who have that experience. You know,

00:36:24.190 --> 00:36:26.710
you've got to do enough
research so that you can ask

00:36:26.710 --> 00:36:29.352
the right questions. And it

00:36:30.710 --> 00:36:31.520
was pretty interesting.

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:33.280
It was a lot of fun,
to be honest with you.

00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:38.740
But it was surprising to
figure out exactly, in the process,

00:36:38.740 --> 00:36:42.010
what you needed to look for on
a resume to get the right level

00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:45.460
of experience because
having some space-y

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:49.000
or some hugging face
experience doesn't necessarily mean that

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:50.680
you were right
for the role. And

00:36:51.490 --> 00:36:54.160
the thing that I think
surprised me most was that when

00:36:54.160 --> 00:36:57.460
we started looking for people
who didn't just have a master's

00:36:57.460 --> 00:37:00.370
degree in data science or
analytics but were looking for

00:37:00.370 --> 00:37:01.825
things that were kind of more,

00:37:02.770 --> 00:37:06.830
like, applied roles
where you were getting

00:37:07.630 --> 00:37:10.480
a master's in or a
PhD in something besides those

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:13.940
particular fields. But you
had to use machine learning,

00:37:13.940 --> 00:37:16.160
data science, NLP
in order to actually

00:37:17.090 --> 00:37:19.700
achieve that PhD
or that master's.

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:22.710
There was a lot of--

00:37:22.710 --> 00:37:26.400
I found those conversations
to be surprisingly useful because

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:27.900
this person is not just--

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:29.955
I went through

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:34.920
a master's program and got
a master's in analytics. And for

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:36.935
the capstone, it was like, OK,

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:39.510
you get to figure out how
to put all of this together,

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:42.270
and then do it on your
own and figure it out.

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:44.970
You took all these classes
and you get to figure it out.

00:37:44.970 --> 00:37:47.490
And people with kind of,

00:37:47.490 --> 00:37:50.790
what I'm calling an
applied perspective for data science

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:53.700
have already been able to
do that. And it's just--

00:37:53.700 --> 00:38:00.150
it was really useful to suss
that out in the process. So

00:38:00.150 --> 00:38:03.660
that's something I'd
recommend is maybe expanding

00:38:05.090 --> 00:38:08.210
the pool of applicants past
just those people that have the

00:38:08.210 --> 00:38:11.450
master's degrees in analytics and
data science that I think

00:38:11.450 --> 00:38:15.580
we probably all have seen
them or have those ourselves.

00:38:16.730 --> 00:38:17.230
- Yeah.

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:21.440
So did you end up redesigning
the job description a bit?

00:38:22.610 --> 00:38:23.672
- No. We

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:26.495
just ended up

00:38:27.230 --> 00:38:29.210
taking a while to find
the right candidate.

