00:42:56.580 --> 00:42:57.765
Want to jump in here next?

00:42:58.420 --> 00:43:00.780
- Oh. Yeah, sure. Hi,
everyone. So basically,

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:03.840
I'm trying to relate to
something I'm currently going

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:07.530
through at work where we just
set up an analytics team.

00:43:08.130 --> 00:43:10.860
And before the business is
willing to really commit any

00:43:10.860 --> 00:43:12.930
resources and invest,
we keep getting this

00:43:13.980 --> 00:43:17.960
ask to estimate the ROI we
expect from the project before

00:43:17.960 --> 00:43:21.380
the business is willing to
commit any time or resources to

00:43:21.380 --> 00:43:22.970
help us with it. And basically,

00:43:22.970 --> 00:43:25.415
I'd like to ask you for
idea around how do you

00:43:26.060 --> 00:43:29.660
approach estimating the ROI from
a project before you even

00:43:29.660 --> 00:43:33.710
really have the resources
or the buy in to start

00:43:34.340 --> 00:43:36.800
looking into it? And
follow-up question,

00:43:36.800 --> 00:43:38.450
once a project is implemented,

00:43:38.450 --> 00:43:40.970
how do you look back and
estimate the ROI from a project

00:43:40.970 --> 00:43:42.450
you've implemented already?

00:43:42.450 --> 00:43:42.610
-

00:43:42.610 --> 00:43:46.535
So this is a really challenging
question to ask because we are

00:43:47.060 --> 00:43:50.730
a nonprofit with a mission to make

00:43:51.410 --> 00:43:54.860
sure that the industry performs
at the highest standards of

00:43:54.860 --> 00:43:55.955
