00:43:56.690 --> 00:44:02.160
So coming up with an ROI,
it's really hard to quantify that.

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:04.190
And we have different
ways that we do that.

00:44:04.190 --> 00:44:07.010
But I don't really maybe
think that they're more generally

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:09.110
applicable to what
you're talking about

00:44:10.070 --> 00:44:13.030
from a pure project
management standpoint.

00:44:13.850 --> 00:44:16.425
Ideally, I feel like what
you would want to do is

00:44:18.420 --> 00:44:19.830
put together--

00:44:22.140 --> 00:44:23.740
I was going to try
to answer it well.

00:44:23.740 --> 00:44:27.010
But I don't have a good answer
for you. Yeah, it's just--

00:44:27.010 --> 00:44:29.095
it's not something
that we deal with

00:44:29.890 --> 00:44:31.600
in the same way that
I think that you do.

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:33.370
And I don't feel like I've
got a good answer for you.

00:44:34.270 --> 00:44:35.884
- I could share one from--

00:44:36.880 --> 00:44:41.560
I could share one from another
hangout we had with Natalie O'Shea

00:44:42.400 --> 00:44:43.135
at BetterUp.

00:44:44.230 --> 00:44:46.675
I was trying to find the little
clip to share with you. But

00:44:47.290 --> 00:44:50.230
Natalie shared a bit how when
she was going through this

00:44:50.230 --> 00:44:53.710
process for getting
approval for Posit Team, she

00:44:54.490 --> 00:44:59.770
focused in on one specific problem
that the team had. And so

00:45:01.030 --> 00:45:03.860
the use case she gave was they're

00:45:04.540 --> 00:45:06.115
a consulting
organization, so their

00:45:06.760 --> 00:45:09.970
sales team had to make
these PowerPoints over and over again

00:45:09.970 --> 00:45:12.880
with new data and based
on different industries.

00:45:12.880 --> 00:45:16.780
And to actually make a
great deck for a presentation was

00:45:16.780 --> 00:45:18.800
taking hours and hours.

00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:19.495
And so

00:45:20.850 --> 00:45:24.960
they sent out a poll to all
of the sales reps to ask how much

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:28.200
time were they taking
building these.

00:45:28.200 --> 00:45:32.400
And they actually put a dollar
amount to it. And so I think

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:37.335
it ended up being like $1.2
million a year in people cost.

00:45:38.610 --> 00:45:42.420
And then she showed how
she could automate that with a

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:46.080
Shiny application that the sales
team would go to the

00:45:46.080 --> 00:45:49.380
Shiny app and put in
whatever they needed for the customer,

00:45:49.380 --> 00:45:52.380
and then automatically
generate the PowerPoint,

00:45:52.380 --> 00:45:55.215
which was tied directly
to their database as well.

00:45:57.210 --> 00:45:58.980
And using that one example,

00:45:58.980 --> 00:46:03.435
she was able to put a
dollar amount to it. So maybe that's

00:46:04.170 --> 00:46:05.655
one tip is to think

00:46:06.810 --> 00:46:09.900
when you're building this
business case to think about

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:13.830
one team that you might be
able to help because then they might

00:46:13.830 --> 00:46:17.550
be the ones kind of joining
on to your business case too.

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:21.190
I think it was maybe the
head of sales that ended up helping

00:46:21.190 --> 00:46:23.935
push it forward for
their data team too.

00:46:24.770 --> 00:46:27.220
- I think that's a very useful
insight actually, Rachel.

00:46:27.220 --> 00:46:31.330
Thanks for that. Only thing is
that I think when it comes to

00:46:31.330 --> 00:46:32.900
automation of

00:46:33.580 --> 00:46:36.376
daily routine tasks
and activities, it's

00:46:37.150 --> 00:46:41.206
a bit easier to estimate
ROI as opposed to analytics

00:46:42.430 --> 00:46:44.830
modeling where you're dealing
with uncertainty, right?

00:46:44.830 --> 00:46:47.290
And sometimes you're not even
sure if you'll get anywhere

00:46:47.290 --> 00:46:49.580
with the time you
invest, right? So

00:46:50.320 --> 00:46:52.930
yeah, it's not an easy task.

00:46:52.930 --> 00:46:55.870
And I would really appreciate
any thoughts or feedback from

00:46:55.870 --> 00:46:58.805
anyone that's gone through that
experience before as well. Cheers.

00:46:59.920 --> 00:47:02.320
- Nick, this is
Eduardo Castillo.

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:06.070
And I actually am from the
nuclear industry. I know, Dave,

00:47:06.070 --> 00:47:10.600
we are working group at INPO.
And one thing I can offer from

00:47:10.600 --> 00:47:14.560
a data science perspective is
that it's important to kind of

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:16.040
go for big targets.

00:47:17.090 --> 00:47:20.170
So one of the big projects
we're working on right now is around

00:47:20.890 --> 00:47:25.840
reducing diving frequencies
for cleaning some of our intake

00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:30.460
structures. And that's a very
big $1 million a year project

00:47:30.460 --> 00:47:35.950
where we had no ongoing
data science efforts to improve

00:47:35.950 --> 00:47:37.277
performance of that

00:47:38.840 --> 00:47:41.470
evolution. And so having
a big target. I think,

00:47:41.470 --> 00:47:45.570
was important because
even if we could cut 5%

00:47:46.390 --> 00:47:50.040
of that, it becomes
a good hard dollar--

00:47:50.040 --> 00:47:54.295
dollar ROI. And
the second piece is

00:47:55.060 --> 00:47:57.010
looking at things
like risk. Like,

00:47:57.010 --> 00:47:59.540
when you have an
activity like diving,

00:47:59.540 --> 00:48:01.820
you do have a
lot of occupational risk.

00:48:01.820 --> 00:48:04.934
And we're actually
able to tie what

00:48:06.830 --> 00:48:09.620
the occupational risk is
and how it compares to other

00:48:09.620 --> 00:48:11.270
activities that we
do at the plant.

00:48:11.270 --> 00:48:15.170
So it allows us to kind of
capture both the hard savings

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:19.590
and also the qualitative
softer savings. And again,

00:48:19.590 --> 00:48:21.860
just starting with a big
target of project that

00:48:22.820 --> 00:48:26.550
is targeting a big budget,
I think, it's important.

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:27.050
- Thanks, Eduardo.

00:48:27.830 --> 00:48:29.250
- Yeah. Thank you so much.

