00:50:13.140 --> 00:50:14.880
But one of the other questions,

00:50:15.570 --> 00:50:16.665
David, was,

00:50:18.280 --> 00:50:21.660
what is your company's
relationship as relates to the

00:50:21.660 --> 00:50:23.740
interface between
government and industry?

00:50:25.270 --> 00:50:25.770
- So

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:27.570
that's great.

00:50:27.570 --> 00:50:30.660
So there's the NRC which
is kind of the government

00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:34.260
regulator. And they have
all of their own guidelines.

00:50:34.260 --> 00:50:36.290
They do their own
sorts of evaluations.

00:50:37.860 --> 00:50:38.782
But the

00:50:40.200 --> 00:50:40.700
whole

00:50:41.250 --> 00:50:45.630
impetus of INFO was when Three
Mile Island happened, there was this--

00:50:45.630 --> 00:50:47.460
Jimmy Carter put
together this commission,

00:50:47.460 --> 00:50:49.180
it was called the
Kemeny Commission. And

00:50:49.920 --> 00:50:52.390
the commission had a
whole lot of findings.

00:50:52.390 --> 00:50:54.900
One of them was that there
needed to be kind of an

00:50:54.900 --> 00:50:58.230
independent non-governmental organization
to set these sorts

00:50:58.230 --> 00:51:01.447
of safety and reliability standards,
and then to assess them.

00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:05.640
So we are not really--

00:51:06.690 --> 00:51:08.980
I mean, we're in the nuclear
industry. So of course,

00:51:08.980 --> 00:51:10.630
we're associated with the NRC.

00:51:10.630 --> 00:51:13.800
But we don't really
answer to them.

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:16.440
We kind of answer more
so to our members,

00:51:16.440 --> 00:51:18.400
to the domestic
nuclear industry.

00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:23.545
So we're two regulatory
bodies that have maybe some

00:51:24.790 --> 00:51:26.200
overlapping interests but also

00:51:26.830 --> 00:51:29.425
some kind of
different missions.

00:51:30.040 --> 00:51:30.760
- Thank you.

00:51:32.240 --> 00:51:36.328
And so a question that I love to
get to ask everybody towards the end is

00:51:37.960 --> 00:51:42.220
there a piece of career advice
that stands out to you, whether

00:51:42.220 --> 00:51:45.880
it's been something that you've
received or advice you've given

00:51:45.880 --> 00:51:47.440
that you'd like
to share with us?

00:51:50.030 --> 00:51:51.030
- I know you were
going to ask this.

00:51:51.030 --> 00:51:52.550
So I had to think
about it in advance.

00:51:52.550 --> 00:51:55.220
And I had like five or six
different things that I came up with.

00:51:55.220 --> 00:52:00.170
The one that I think I want
to stick with is be open to doing

00:52:00.170 --> 00:52:01.730
things that make you
feel uncomfortable.

00:52:03.540 --> 00:52:06.240
So as a human being but
also professionally,

00:52:06.240 --> 00:52:08.970
that's kind of where
the growth happens.

00:52:08.970 --> 00:52:11.100
If you just do the same
thing day in and day out,

00:52:11.100 --> 00:52:12.930
you're just going
to kind of Tread

00:52:13.830 --> 00:52:15.850
water in a way.

00:52:15.850 --> 00:52:19.500
But if you lean into the
things that make you uncomfortable in

00:52:19.500 --> 00:52:21.090
a healthy way, obviously,

00:52:21.090 --> 00:52:24.818
then I think that's
where you can grow your

00:52:28.030 --> 00:52:31.870
professional relationships, your
technical capabilities, like,

00:52:31.870 --> 00:52:35.950
your mental health in a way
is kind of leaning into that and

00:52:35.950 --> 00:52:38.080
being OK with the discomfort,

00:52:38.080 --> 00:52:40.720
knowing that you're going
to learn something from it.

00:52:41.360 --> 00:52:45.270
- So can you share an
example with us of where

00:52:46.210 --> 00:52:47.200
you're doing that?

00:52:47.200 --> 00:52:48.685
- Being asked to do a
Data Science Hangout?

00:52:49.510 --> 00:52:50.010
- Yes.

00:52:51.130 --> 00:52:51.730
Awesome.

00:52:52.300 --> 00:52:53.245
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:52:55.180 --> 00:52:56.890
And the other piece.

00:52:57.490 --> 00:52:59.875
And I've been a part
of them for seven years

00:53:00.520 --> 00:53:01.590
is Toastmasters.

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:06.460
Working on your communication skills
is critical as a data

00:53:06.460 --> 00:53:08.905
scientist. You can
create the coolest

00:53:10.000 --> 00:53:10.500
model,

00:53:11.740 --> 00:53:13.700
next big app, that
sort of thing.

00:53:13.700 --> 00:53:15.160
But if you can't really
communicate it well,

00:53:15.160 --> 00:53:19.090
then you're going to have
a hard time getting other people

00:53:19.090 --> 00:53:21.060
to understand why
it's so important. So

00:53:21.730 --> 00:53:25.310
figuring out ways to kind
of hone those skills are really,

00:53:25.310 --> 00:53:28.595
really important just as much in
my opinion as the technical skills.

00:53:29.360 --> 00:53:31.220
- Definitely. Yani,

00:53:32.210 --> 00:53:35.510
I see you had asked a question
in the chat want to jump in here.

